---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
    title="Come Home — a family film"
    description="An epic feel-good short film built from our Geni GEDCOM file: Poland and Latvia to Chicago, England, South Africa, and Toronto — and now home to Israel. Made with Manim, Remotion, and GSAP/Puppeteer."
>
    <main class="container mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 sm:py-16">
        <article class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert mx-auto max-w-3xl">
            <header class="mb-10 text-center not-prose">
                <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-text-light dark:text-text-dark sm:text-5xl">
                    Come Home
                </h1>
                <p class="mt-3 text-xl italic text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">
                    Poland &middot; Latvia &middot; Chicago &middot; England &middot; South Africa &middot; Toronto &middot; Israel
                </p>
            </header>

            <div class="not-prose mb-12">
                <video
                    controls
                    preload="metadata"
                    poster="/family/poster.jpg"
                    class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-2xl"
                >
                    <source src="/family/come-home.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
                    Your browser does not support the video tag.
                </video>
                <p class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-gray-500">7m21s &middot; 720p</p>
            </div>

            <h2>The brief</h2>
            <p>
                I gave Claude (Anthropic's Sonnet 5, running in Claude Code) our family's exported
                Geni GEDCOM file and one instruction: turn 150 years of scattering — Poland, Latvia,
                England, Chicago, South Africa, Toronto — into an epic feel-good short film, tie it to
                Tisha B'Av, and end with the five of us coming home to Israel. Build it with Manim,
                Remotion, and whatever else fit.
            </p>
            <p>
                Everything below — parsing the GEDCOM, tracing the actual ancestor chains, downloading
                the real archival photographs and documents, writing the shot list, building three
                separate render pipelines, and cutting the final film — happened in a single agent
                session.
            </p>

            <h2>The real story</h2>
            <p>
                The film isn't invented — every place and date comes straight out of the GEDCOM.
                Matthew's line traces to <strong>Icek Melcer &amp; Marya Pfefer</strong>, a flour
                trader's family in Połaniec, Poland, whose descendants eventually settled in Toronto.
                Georgia's line traces to <strong>Mordechai Bennowitz</strong> (a tailor) and
                <strong>Hanna Engelberg</strong> of Bausk, Latvia — Georgia's great-great-great-grandparents —
                whose children scattered three ways: most, including Mordechai and Hanna themselves,
                to <strong>Chicago</strong> (they're buried together at Waldheim Cemetery), one daughter,
                Beile, to Hull, England, and one son, Jacob, to London and then South Africa.
            </p>
            <p>
                Matthew and Georgia's marriage reunites those threads. Aviya, Tzofia, and Lielle — three
                daughters, the same number that once scattered the family — are the generation bringing
                it home. The film frames the whole arc through Tisha B'Av: the fast that mourns the
                scattering, and "Nachamu" — the comfort that follows it.
            </p>

            <h2>The real archival material</h2>
            <p>
                Pulled directly from the media links in the GEDCOM (all originally sourced from Geni.com
                and Polish civil archives):
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>The Bennowitz family portrait, Riga, 1880, and Mordechai &amp; Hanna as elders, c. 1900</li>
                <li>Jacob Benowitz's 1899 London marriage register — which names his father as "Marcus" (Mordechai) Bennowitz, tailor</li>
                <li>The 1911 England census listing "Jack Wenowitz," tailor, in Paddington</li>
                <li>Family birth certificates from Chicago, 1900s</li>
                <li>The 1880 Połaniec civil register recording the birth of Chaim Herszel Meller</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Tools used</h2>
            <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
                    <thead>
                        <tr class="border-b border-gray-300 dark:border-gray-700 text-left">
                            <th class="py-2 pr-4">Pipeline</th>
                            <th class="py-2 pr-4">What it built</th>
                            <th class="py-2">Version</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-800">
                        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">Manim Community</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Migration maps, family-tree diagrams, the timeline strip</td><td class="py-2">0.20.1</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">Remotion</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Archival photo compositing (Ken Burns), illustrated title cards</td><td class="py-2">4.0.496</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">GSAP + Puppeteer</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Tisha B'Av bookends (Hebrew calligraphic text, candle &amp; sunrise motifs)</td><td class="py-2">GSAP 3.15.0, Puppeteer 23.11.1</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">ffmpeg</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Crossfade assembly, score mix, final encode</td><td class="py-2">8.1.2</td></tr>
                        <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">Python + NumPy/SciPy</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">Original ambient score — synthesized, no samples or loops</td><td class="py-2">3.12.9</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p>
                Motion Canvas was the first choice for the Tisha B'Av bookends — it's the closest
                web-native analogue to Manim's scene-graph approach — but its headless render path
                wasn't reliably scriptable in the time available, so the agent fell back to GSAP driving
                plain HTML/SVG, captured frame-by-frame with headless Puppeteer. Three genuinely distinct
                animation paradigms in one film: Python scene graph, React-frame compositing, and DOM/SVG
                timeline tweening.
            </p>

            <h2>Why it works</h2>
            <p>
                Every place name, date, and document in this film is real — pulled from our own family
                tree, not generated. The tools just gave 150 years of wandering a camera, and a way home.
            </p>
            <p class="italic">
                Georgia, Matthew, Aviya, Tzofia, Lielle — this year, in Jerusalem.
            </p>
        </article>
    </main>
</Layout>
